export default "## Why This Is Genuinely Confusing\n\nImplantation bleeding and a period can occur at nearly the same time in the cycle. If your cycle is 28 days with a 14-day luteal phase, your period is expected around day 28. Implantation, if it occurs, typically happens somewhere between days 20 and 26. The timing overlap is real. The early symptoms of pregnancy, fatigue, breast tenderness, mild cramping, are also symptoms that appear in the late luteal phase regardless of whether conception occurred.\n\nThe key is to look at several characteristics together rather than relying on any single factor.\n\nThis guide is for educational purposes only and is not a substitute for medical advice.\n\n## Timing\n\n**Implantation bleeding** arrives earlier than your expected period, typically 4 to 10 days before menstruation would be due. It occurs when the embryo embeds into the uterine lining, disrupting a small number of blood vessels in the process.\n\n**A period** arrives on or close to the expected date, though cycle variation means \"on time\" spans a range. If bleeding arrives very close to the expected date or later, implantation bleeding becomes a less likely explanation.\n\nThis is why tracking your cycle matters. If you do not know when your period is typically expected, you cannot meaningfully assess whether bleeding is early.\n\n## Color and Appearance\n\n**Implantation bleeding** is most commonly pink or brownish. The brownish color occurs because the blood takes longer to travel from the implantation site to outside the body, during which it oxidizes and darkens. Pink spotting suggests fresher blood but still a small amount.\n\n**A period** typically starts with light brownish spotting or pink, then transitions to bright red as flow increases. The progression from light to heavier and redder is characteristic of menstruation.\n\nBright red, heavy bleeding from the start is not consistent with implantation bleeding.\n\n## Flow Volume\n\n**Implantation bleeding** is light, the defining characteristic. We are talking about spots on underwear, light staining when wiping, or at most a pantyliner amount. It does not fill pads or tampons. It does not involve clots.\n\n**A period** builds. Even for people with light periods, there is a noticeable increase in flow over the first day or two that reaches a peak, then tapers. Clots are common, particularly in heavier flow.\n\nIf bleeding is filling more than a panty liner per day, it is much more likely to be a period than implantation bleeding.\n\n## Duration\n\n**Implantation bleeding** typically lasts one to three days. Some people notice it only once, a single instance of spotting that does not recur.\n\n**A period** typically lasts three to seven days (with significant individual variation). A period that lasts fewer than two days is worth tracking as potentially abnormal, but is still more consistent with a light period than with implantation bleeding if the timing and flow match cycle expectations.\n\n## Accompanying Symptoms\n\n**Implantation bleeding** may come with mild cramping, lighter and shorter than typical menstrual cramps. Some people also report mild nausea, breast tenderness, and fatigue at this stage, though these early pregnancy symptoms overlap substantially with late luteal phase PMS symptoms.\n\n**A period** typically involves cramping that is stronger and more sustained, particularly on the first one to two days. Period cramps are usually caused by prostaglandins triggering uterine contractions, which feels different from the mild, brief cramping some people associate with implantation.\n\n## When to Take a Pregnancy Test\n\nThe most straightforward way to distinguish implantation bleeding from a period: wait until the expected period date has passed and take a pregnancy test. By then, if implantation occurred, hCG levels are usually sufficient to produce a positive result on a standard home test.\n\nTesting during or right after suspected implantation bleeding may give a false negative, because hCG may not yet be detectable. Sensitive early-result tests can sometimes detect pregnancy a few days before a missed period, but testing too early produces false negatives even in confirmed pregnancies.\n\nIf you get a positive test, some light bleeding in early pregnancy is common and often not a concern, but early pregnancy bleeding should be discussed with a healthcare provider, particularly if it is heavier, one-sided, or accompanied by significant pain.\n\n## What to Log\n\nWhen you notice spotting of any kind, record:\n- Date and time\n- Color (pink, brown, red)\n- Amount (trace, light, moderate)\n- Whether it recurred the following day\n- Any accompanying symptoms (cramping, breast tenderness, nausea)\n- Where you are in your cycle relative to your expected period date\n\nThis record is useful whether you are trying to conceive, trying not to, or simply trying to understand your cycle.\n\n## The Privacy Angle\n\nTracking reproductive cycle data, including spotting, pregnancy suspicion, and pregnancy testing, is deeply private information. In states where abortion is criminalized, this data could become legally significant. Period tracking apps that store data on servers can be compelled to provide it in response to legal subpoenas. Period app data is not protected by HIPAA, which applies to healthcare providers and insurers, not consumer apps.\n\nFlo Health settled an FTC enforcement action in 2021 after sharing user health data with third parties including Facebook and Google. A $59.5 million class action settlement followed in 2025. These are not theoretical concerns.\n\nAn on-device tracker never transmits your data to a company server. There is no database to subpoena and no third-party data sharing pipeline. If you are tracking cycle data that includes pregnancy-related information, where that data lives is not an abstract question.";
